---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

const dist = [
  ["United States", "6,242", "1:58,068", "6,880"],
  ["Ireland", "3,101", "1:1,519", "304"],
  ["Australia", "2,897", "1:9,319", "1,394"],
  ["France", "1,591", "1:41,749", "5,245"],
  ["England", "1,430", "1:38,964", "5,048"],
  ["Yemen", "1,081", "1:24,445", "2,460"],
  ["Canada", "537", "1:68,614", "7,893"],
];
---
<Base
  title="The Name"
  eyebrow="Two origins, no common ancestor"
  dek="D'Arcy was coined twice over — once in Normandy as the name of a place, once in Ireland as the name of a colour. Anyone searching the name needs to know that before they start.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">There is <strong>no single founding D'Arcy family</strong>. The name reaches the
    English-speaking world by two entirely independent routes, and this is a method problem before it
    is a history.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Norman — the name of a place</h3>
        <p><em>d'Arcy</em> is French for <strong>"of Arcy"</strong>: a man named for the land he held.
        The place is usually given as <strong>Arcy in La Manche, Normandy</strong>; some accounts point
        instead to <strong>Bois-d'Arcy</strong> in the Yvelines, west of Paris. A
        <em>Norman de Areci</em> appears as a tenant-in-chief in Lincolnshire in the reign of William I.
        By this route the name is <strong>territorial</strong>. It tells you where a family held land,
        and nothing whatever about what they were like.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Gaelic — the name of a colour</h3>
        <p><em>Darcy</em>, characteristically <strong>without</strong> the apostrophe, is an
        anglicisation of <strong>Ó Dorchaidhe</strong>, from <em>dorcha</em>, "dark" — dark-haired,
        dark-complexioned, or figuratively secretive. It is a nickname name, and there is nothing
        Norman about it at all.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Note>
      <strong>The apostrophe is a hint and not a proof.</strong> It leans Norman, and it was added,
      dropped and re-added by clerks, priests, recruiting sergeants and shipping agents for four
      hundred years. Our own family is written both ways across its own records — and its most
      distinctive given name, <em>Lindesay</em>, is spelled <em>Lindsay</em> in the very next
      generation. <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>See the spine →</a>
    </Note>
  </section>

  <Blk label="The most misused fact in D'Arcy genealogy" title="The Tribes of Galway">
    <p>The seventeenth-century genealogist <strong>Dubhaltach MacFhirbhisigh</strong> records that
    <strong>Walter Riabhach Ó Dorchaidhe</strong> (fl. <em>c.</em> 1488) was "the first man of the Uí
    Dorchaidhe who came to Galway, according to the Galweigians themselves". His descendants became
    <strong>D'Arcy</strong>, and D'Arcy is counted among the <strong>Tribes of Galway</strong>, the
    fourteen merchant families who ran that city.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">This matters because of how it is habitually used. The Galway D'Arcys
    are <strong>Gaelic Ó Dorchaidhe who adopted a Norman-looking spelling</strong>. They are not the
    Norman de Arcy of Lincolnshire. A pedigree that climbs to "the D'Arcys of Galway" and then steps
    across to Normandy has crossed between two unrelated families at exactly the point where the
    record is thinnest — and it is a step people take constantly, because it makes the better
    story.</p>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Distribution</h2>
    <p class="dek">Forebears, spelling <em>Darcy</em>, accessed 9 September 2026: <strong>17,796
    bearers worldwide</strong>, the 30,038th most common surname on earth, incidence 1:409,505.
    Variants <em>D'arcy · Darcey · Darchy · Dàrcy</em> are counted separately, so the true total for
    the name-group is higher.</p>
    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th class="m">Bearers</th><th class="m">Density</th><th class="m">Rank there</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {dist.map(([c, b, d, r]) => (
            <tr><td>{c}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td class="m">{d}</td><td class="m">{r}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <h3 style="margin-top:1.6rem">Read the table with three cautions</h3>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li><strong>Ireland is the centre of gravity, not America.</strong> The United States has the
      most bearers; Ireland has them at thirty-eight times the density and ranks the name 304th
      nationally. On density Ireland is the homeland and the rest is diaspora.</li>
      <li><strong>Australia at 1:9,319 is a serious presence</strong> — denser than England, and six
      times denser than the United States. That is the pool this family sits in, and it is consistent
      with Irish emigration to the colonies rather than with an English military family. Ours is the
      second kind, which makes it unusual for the country it landed in.</li>
      <li><strong>Yemen is almost certainly not this name at all</strong>, but a transliteration
      collision from Arabic. It must not be folded into an Irish-Norman story.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What follows for this archive</h2>
    <p class="dek">Our line is <strong>English and military</strong>, not Irish and agricultural: it
    reaches Australia through Chatham, the Royal Engineers town, in the uniform of the 39th Foot. That
    is documented. Whether it reaches further back to Norman Yorkshire is a separate question and a
    much weaker one. <a href={u("/hornby/")}>The Hornby claim →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
